I think it would be worth putting a guard on the code, like
```
const maxDeviceIndex = 1024
if *eni.Attachment.DeviceIndex  > maxDeviceIndex {
  return Error(...)
}
```

I withdraw the suggestion to use a map, but a comment here about the desire to retain gaps as "" would be good.

The DeviceIndex cannot be greater than the maximum number of network interfaces minus one (the DeviceIndex starts from zero). So it cannot be greater than 79 in case someone has enough money to afford the `trn1n.32xlarge` instance.
I created a small `t4g.small` instance with 3 network interfaces. I detached the last of them (DeviceIndex=2) and tried to attach that again with DeviceIndex=3 but it failed with `AttachmentLimitExceeded`. It succeeded with DeviceIndex=2.
```
[ec2-user@i-09fd32b04973c0de7 ~]$ aws ec2 describe-instances --instance-ids i-09fd32b04973c0de7 | jq '.Reservations[0].Instances[0].NetworkInterfaces[] | { "ENI": .NetworkInterfaceId, "DeviceIndex": .Attachment.DeviceIndex, "AttachmentId": .Attachment.AttachmentId }'
{
  "ENI": "eni-019197b6acfcf0dd3",
  "DeviceIndex": 1,
  "AttachmentId": "eni-attach-0be9e07d6fe70015b"
}
{
  "ENI": "eni-076b468329cc12226",
  "DeviceIndex": 2,
  "AttachmentId": "eni-attach-07ddccc8bdc7a3c4c"
}
{
  "ENI": "eni-0f29c52f56da93ec8",
  "DeviceIndex": 0,
  "AttachmentId": "eni-attach-08e894e40c9281afa"
}
[ec2-user@i-09fd32b04973c0de7 ~]$ aws ec2 detach-network-interface --attachment-id eni-attach-07ddccc8bdc7a3c4c
[ec2-user@i-09fd32b04973c0de7 ~]$ aws ec2 describe-instances --instance-ids i-09fd32b04973c0de7 | jq '.Reservations[0].Instances[0].NetworkInterfaces[] | { "ENI": .NetworkInterfaceId, "DeviceIndex": .Attachment.DeviceIndex, "AttachmentId": .Attachment.AttachmentId }'
{
  "ENI": "eni-019197b6acfcf0dd3",
  "DeviceIndex": 1,
  "AttachmentId": "eni-attach-0be9e07d6fe70015b"
}
{
  "ENI": "eni-0f29c52f56da93ec8",
  "DeviceIndex": 0,
  "AttachmentId": "eni-attach-08e894e40c9281afa"
}
[ec2-user@i-09fd32b04973c0de7 ~]$ aws ec2 attach-network-interface --device-index 3 --instance-id i-09fd32b04973c0de7 --network-interface-id eni-076b468329cc12226

An error occurred (AttachmentLimitExceeded) when calling the AttachNetworkInterface operation: Device index 3 exceeds the limit for t4g.small
[ec2-user@i-09fd32b04973c0de7 ~]$ aws ec2 attach-network-interface --device-index 2 --instance-id i-09fd32b04973c0de7 --network-interface-id eni-076b468329cc12226
{
    "AttachmentId": "eni-attach-0ea97696143c99dd2",
    "NetworkCardIndex": 0
}
[ec2-user@i-09fd32b04973c0de7 ~]$ aws ec2 describe-instances --instance-ids i-09fd32b04973c0de7 | jq '.Reservations[0].Instances[0].NetworkInterfaces[] | { "ENI": .NetworkInterfaceId, "DeviceIndex": .Attachment.DeviceIndex, "AttachmentId": .Attachment.AttachmentId }'
{
  "ENI": "eni-019197b6acfcf0dd3",
  "DeviceIndex": 1,
  "AttachmentId": "eni-attach-0be9e07d6fe70015b"
}
{
  "ENI": "eni-0f29c52f56da93ec8",
  "DeviceIndex": 0,
  "AttachmentId": "eni-attach-08e894e40c9281afa"
}
{
  "ENI": "eni-076b468329cc12226",
  "DeviceIndex": 2,
  "AttachmentId": "eni-attach-0ea97696143c99dd2"
}

```
You can see that the order of the interfaces in the response can change. This is why I use the DeviceIndex to, well, index the new label. It doesn't matter if someone detaches an interface and attaches a new one until they use a different ENI as a scraping target. It will remain in the same "position" in the label.
